%U A000360 7,5,6,2,8,5,9,4,8,5,7,1,7,6,9,4,11,6,9,3,8,6,10,3,8,5,5,1,6,4,8,4,9,6,9,2
%N A000360 Distribution of nonempty triangles inside a fractal rep-4-tile.
%C A000360 a(n) is the number of congruent nonempty medial down-triangles in column n of the Gosper-Lafitte triangle, along lines perpendicular to the base. [Enhanced by the author]
%C A000360 Also, a(n) = Sum of the coefficients of the terms with an even exponent in the Stern polynomial B(n+1,t), or in other words, the sum of the even-indexed terms (the leftmost is at index 0) of the irregular triangle A125184, starting from its second row. - _Antti Karttunen_, Apr 20 2017
%C A000360 Back in May 1995, it was proved that a(n) = modulo 3 mapping, (+1,-1,+0)/2, of the Stern-Brocot sequence A002487, dropping its 1st term. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Apr 23 2017
%C A000360 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 28 2026: (Start)
%C A000360 Let D={(0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (-1,-1)}, and let K be the unique nonempty compact set satisfying K = Union_{d in D} (K + d)/2. Put omega = e^(2*Pi*i/3) and define the map L(x,y) = x + omega*y. Since L is linear and sends D to {0,1,omega,omega^2}, L(K) is the Gosper-Lafitte triangle in equilateral normalization, namely the self-replicating tile for the Eisenstein integers with this digit set. Multiplication by omega is a symmetry of the entire iterated construction: it permutes the digit set and the corresponding contraction maps. Hence the attractor and its recursive decomposition have threefold rotational symmetry. The canonical topological decomposition starts from three overlapping portions of side length 2/3, related by 2*Pi/3 rotations. Within every such up-triangular portion arising recursively in this decomposition, the axial child retains the type, filled or empty, of its parent, while the two lateral children receive the opposite type. The tile is the closure of the union of all filled medial down-triangles.
%C A000360 Consider fusc(n)=A002487(n), empt(n)=A284556(n+1), and let chi_3(n)=A102283(n) be the Dirichlet character modulo 3. The modulo 3 mapping above can be reexpressed as 2*a(n) - fusc(n+1) = a(n) - empt(n) = chi_3(n+1). For column n in the underlying L(K), fusc(n+1) gives the total count of congruent triangles, of which the complementary counts a(n) and empt(n) correspond respectively to the nonempty and empty cases.
%C A000360 The Gosper-Lafitte triangle thus offers a geometric realization of Moritz Abraham Stern's 1858 enumeration of the positive rational numbers through ratios of successive column counts in the triangle. See A002487.
%C A000360 At dyadic level r, the count profile from the left extremity to the central axis is a(0), ..., a(3*2^r - 1). These finite profiles persist under refinement: the level-r profile is the initial half of the level-(r+1) profile. Thus a(n) is the stable column-count profile obtained under successive refinements. The paired filled-empty profile is a fixed point of the operator given below.
%C A000360 Let F(x) and E(x) be the respective generating functions of a(n) and empt(n), then {{F(x)}, {E(x)}} = {{1 + x^2, x},{x, 1 + x^2}}*{{F(x^2)}, {E(x^2)}}, with F(0)=1 and E(0)=0. (End)
%D A000360 M. J. Lafitte, Sur l'Effet Noah en Géométrie, rapport à l'INPI, mars 1995.
%H A000360 T. D. Noe, <a href="/A000360/b000360.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A000360 S. Klavzar, U. Milutinovic and C. Petr, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aam.2006.01.003">Stern polynomials</a>, Adv. Appl. Math. 39 (2007) 86-95.
